1
00:04:32,862 --> 00:04:35,615
<i>Next subject: Kowalski, Leon.</i>

2
00:04:36,032 --> 00:04:37,992
<i>Engineer, waste disposal.</i>

3
00:04:38,201 --> 00:04:41,078
<i>File section: New employee, six days.</i>

4
00:04:42,455 --> 00:04:43,873
<i>Calling Mr. Webber...</i>

5
00:04:44,040 --> 00:04:46,876
- Come in.
<i>- ...please report to Zone A Sector 9.</i>

6
00:04:50,421 --> 00:04:54,842
<i>Replication Center Level 9,
we have a B1 security alert.</i>

7
00:04:55,009 --> 00:04:57,595
<i>Stand by for ID check, please.</i>

8
00:04:58,221 --> 00:04:59,263
Sit down.

9
00:04:59,889 --> 00:05:03,059
<i>Replication Center Level 9,
we have a B1 security alert.</i>

10
00:05:03,226 --> 00:05:04,268
<i>Stand by for....</i>

11
00:05:04,435 --> 00:05:05,728
Care if I talk?

12
00:05:06,229 --> 00:05:08,064
I'm kind of nervous when I take tests.

13
00:05:08,231 --> 00:05:10,066
Just please don't move.

14
00:05:11,234 --> 00:05:12,902
Oh, sorry.

15
00:05:16,239 --> 00:05:19,242
I already had an IQ test.
I don't think I've had one of these.

16
00:05:19,408 --> 00:05:21,661
Reaction time is a factor,
so pay attention.

17
00:05:21,828 --> 00:05:24,413
Now, answer as quickly as you can.

18
00:05:24,914 --> 00:05:26,415
Sure.

19
00:05:27,416 --> 00:05:29,085
1187 at Hunterwasser.

20
00:05:29,252 --> 00:05:30,253
That's the hotel.

21
00:05:30,419 --> 00:05:32,588
- What?
- Where I live.

22
00:05:32,755 --> 00:05:34,423
- Nice place?
- Yeah, sure, I guess.

23
00:05:34,590 --> 00:05:36,259
Is that part of the test?

24
00:05:36,425 --> 00:05:39,095
No. Just warming you up. That's all.

25
00:05:40,054 --> 00:05:42,306
It's not fancy or anything.

26
00:05:43,432 --> 00:05:46,018
You're in a desert,
walking along in the sand, when...

27
00:05:46,185 --> 00:05:47,270
Is this the test now?

28
00:05:47,436 --> 00:05:49,939
Yes. You're in a desert,
walking along in the sand...

29
00:05:50,106 --> 00:05:51,816
- ...when you look down...
- What one?

30
00:05:51,983 --> 00:05:53,734
- What?
- What desert?

31
00:05:53,901 --> 00:05:56,696
Doesn't make any difference.
It's completely hypothetical.

32
00:05:56,863 --> 00:05:59,240
- But how come I'd be there?
- Maybe you're fed up.

33
00:05:59,407 --> 00:06:01,701
Maybe you wanna be by yourself.
Who knows?

34
00:06:02,368 --> 00:06:03,995
You look down and see a tortoise.

35
00:06:04,161 --> 00:06:07,790
- It's crawling towards you...
- Tortoise? What's that?

36
00:06:10,877 --> 00:06:13,296
- You know what a turtle is?
- Of course.

37
00:06:13,462 --> 00:06:15,047
Same thing.

38
00:06:15,214 --> 00:06:16,799
I've never seen a turtle.

39
00:06:19,468 --> 00:06:20,970
But I understand what you mean.

40
00:06:21,137 --> 00:06:23,806
You reach down,
you flip the tortoise over on its back.

41
00:06:23,973 --> 00:06:26,350
Do you make up these questions,
Mr. Holden?

42
00:06:26,517 --> 00:06:28,644
Or do they write them down for you?

43
00:06:28,811 --> 00:06:31,772
The tortoise lays on its back,
its belly baking in the sun...

44
00:06:31,939 --> 00:06:34,984
...beating its legs,
trying to turn itself over, but it can't...

45
00:06:35,151 --> 00:06:36,652
...not without your help.

46
00:06:36,819 --> 00:06:39,113
- You're not helping.
- What do you mean I'm not?

47
00:06:39,280 --> 00:06:40,615
I mean, you're not helping.

48
00:06:41,157 --> 00:06:42,241
Why is that, Leon?

49
00:06:50,833 --> 00:06:53,336
They're just questions, Leon.

50
00:06:54,003 --> 00:06:56,881
In answer to your query,
they're written down for me.

51
00:06:57,048 --> 00:07:01,344
It's a test designed to provoke
an emotional response.

52
00:07:03,846 --> 00:07:05,890
Shall we continue?

53
00:07:07,058 --> 00:07:10,853
Describe in single words only the
good things that come into your mind...

54
00:07:11,562 --> 00:07:13,230
...about your mother.

55
00:07:13,397 --> 00:07:15,942
- My mother?
- Yeah.

56
00:07:17,193 --> 00:07:19,695
Let me tell you about my mother.

57
00:07:35,544 --> 00:07:39,548
<i>A new life awaits you
in the Off-world colonies.</i>

58
00:07:39,882 --> 00:07:41,759
<i>The chance to begin again...</i>

59
00:07:41,926 --> 00:07:45,972
<i>...in a golden land of opportunity
and adventure.</i>

60
00:08:11,872 --> 00:08:16,085
<i>A new life awaits you
in the Off-world colonies.</i>

61
00:08:16,293 --> 00:08:18,254
<i>The chance to begin again...</i>

62
00:08:18,421 --> 00:08:22,049
<i>...in a golden land of opportunity
and adventure.</i>

63
00:08:25,428 --> 00:08:27,096
<i>The custom-tailored...</i>

64
00:08:27,263 --> 00:08:29,515
<i>...genetically engineered
humanoid Replicant...</i>

65
00:08:29,682 --> 00:08:32,727
<i>...designed especially for your needs.</i>

66
00:08:32,935 --> 00:08:36,731
<i>So come on, America.
Let's put out....</i>

67
00:08:38,357 --> 00:08:40,067
Give me four.

68
00:08:42,069 --> 00:08:43,863
No, four. Two, two. Four.

69
00:08:47,575 --> 00:08:48,826
And noodles.

70
00:09:02,465 --> 00:09:03,674
Hey.

71
00:09:12,099 --> 00:09:14,685
He say you under arrest, Mr. Deckard.

72
00:09:15,603 --> 00:09:17,063
Got the wrong guy, pal.

73
00:09:22,693 --> 00:09:24,111
He say you Blade Runner.

74
00:09:24,278 --> 00:09:25,738
Tell him I'm eating.

75
00:09:25,905 --> 00:09:27,114
Captain Bryant....

76
00:09:30,868 --> 00:09:32,369
Bryant, huh?

77
00:09:47,718 --> 00:09:49,804
<i>Yellow 3.</i>

78
00:09:50,012 --> 00:09:52,640
<i>Climb and maintain 4000.</i>

79
00:10:55,077 --> 00:10:57,163
<i>Final descent.</i>

80
00:10:57,329 --> 00:11:00,291
<i>Now on glide path. Mark course.</i>

81
00:11:00,457 --> 00:11:02,501
<i>Over the landing threshold.</i>

82
00:11:21,187 --> 00:11:22,229
Hi, Deck.

83
00:11:22,396 --> 00:11:23,689
Bryant.

84
00:11:23,856 --> 00:11:27,151
You wouldn't have come
if I just asked you to. Sit down, pal.

85
00:11:28,611 --> 00:11:32,948
Come on, don't be an asshole, Deckard.
I've got four skin-jobs walking the streets.

86
00:11:49,256 --> 00:11:52,343
They jumped a shuttle Off-world,
killed the crew and passengers.

87
00:11:52,509 --> 00:11:55,554
They found the shuttle drifting
off the coast two weeks ago...

88
00:11:55,721 --> 00:11:57,681
...so we know they're around.

89
00:11:59,016 --> 00:12:00,226
Embarrassing.

90
00:12:00,392 --> 00:12:03,854
No, not embarrassing. No one's ever
gonna find out they're down here.

91
00:12:04,021 --> 00:12:06,607
Because you're gonna spot them
and air them out.

92
00:12:06,774 --> 00:12:08,067
I don't work here anymore.

93
00:12:09,443 --> 00:12:10,527
Give it to Holden.

94
00:12:11,028 --> 00:12:12,988
- He's good.
- I did.

95
00:12:13,155 --> 00:12:15,699
He can breathe okay
as long as nobody unplugs him.

96
00:12:16,533 --> 00:12:19,745
He's not good enough.
Not good as you.

97
00:12:19,912 --> 00:12:21,538
I need you, Deck.

98
00:12:21,705 --> 00:12:24,041
Now, this is a bad one, the worst yet.

99
00:12:24,875 --> 00:12:26,794
I need the old Blade Runner.

100
00:12:26,961 --> 00:12:28,545
I need your magic.

101
00:12:29,880 --> 00:12:32,758
I was quit
when I come in here, Bryant.

102
00:12:33,550 --> 00:12:35,469
I'm twice as quit now.

103
00:12:35,678 --> 00:12:38,555
Stop right where you are.
You know the score, pal?

104
00:12:39,223 --> 00:12:41,475
If you're not cop, you're little people.

105
00:12:53,570 --> 00:12:54,989
No choice, huh?

106
00:12:56,490 --> 00:12:58,492
No choice, pal.

107
00:12:58,784 --> 00:13:01,912
<i>I already had an IQ test this year.</i>

108
00:13:02,079 --> 00:13:04,039
<i>I don't think I've ever
had one of these.</i>

109
00:13:04,206 --> 00:13:06,792
<i>Reaction time is a factor,
so please pay attention.</i>

110
00:13:06,959 --> 00:13:09,086
<i>- Answer as quickly as you can.
- Yeah, sure.</i>

111
00:13:09,253 --> 00:13:12,256
<i>1187 at Hunterwasser.</i>

112
00:13:12,423 --> 00:13:13,799
<i>Yeah. That's the hotel.</i>

113
00:13:13,966 --> 00:13:16,260
<i>- What?
- Where I live.</i>

114
00:13:16,427 --> 00:13:18,637
<i>- Nice place?
- Yeah, sure, I guess.</i>

115
00:13:18,804 --> 00:13:21,807
There was an escape from
the Off-world colonies two weeks ago.

116
00:13:21,974 --> 00:13:24,435
Six Replicants:
three male, three female.

117
00:13:24,601 --> 00:13:27,104
They slaughtered 23 people
and jumped a shuttle.

118
00:13:27,271 --> 00:13:29,606
An aerial patrol spotted
the ship off the coast.

119
00:13:29,773 --> 00:13:31,525
No crew, no sight of them.

120
00:13:31,734 --> 00:13:34,820
Three nights ago they tried to break
into Tyrell Corporation.

121
00:13:34,987 --> 00:13:37,364
Two of them got fried running
through an electrical field.

122
00:13:37,531 --> 00:13:39,450
We lost the others.

123
00:13:40,451 --> 00:13:44,121
On the possibility they might try
to infiltrate as employees...

124
00:13:44,288 --> 00:13:48,459
...I had Holden go over and run
Voight-Kampff tests on the new workers.

125
00:13:48,625 --> 00:13:50,419
Looks like he got himself one.

126
00:13:50,586 --> 00:13:54,214
<i>So you look down and you see
a tortoise. It's crawling towards you.</i>

127
00:13:54,381 --> 00:13:55,549
<i>- Tortoise? What's that?</i>
- That's Leon.

128
00:13:55,716 --> 00:13:57,885
Ammunition loader
on intergalactic runs.

129
00:13:58,052 --> 00:14:01,138
He can lift 400-pound atomic loads
all day and night.

130
00:14:01,305 --> 00:14:03,724
The only way you can hurt him
is to kill him.

131
00:14:03,891 --> 00:14:08,312
I don't get it. What do they risk coming
back to Earth for? That's unusual. Why...?

132
00:14:08,479 --> 00:14:11,190
What do they want
out of the Tyrell Corporation?

133
00:14:11,357 --> 00:14:14,151
Well, you tell me, pal.
That's what you're here for.

134
00:14:25,412 --> 00:14:29,083
- What's this?
- Nexus 6. Roy Batty.

135
00:14:29,291 --> 00:14:31,210
Incept date: 2016.

136
00:14:31,377 --> 00:14:34,129
Combat model.
Optimum self-sufficiency.

137
00:14:35,506 --> 00:14:37,424
Probably the leader.

138
00:14:40,844 --> 00:14:42,513
This is Zhora.

139
00:14:42,679 --> 00:14:45,140
She's trained for
an Off-world kick murder squad.

140
00:14:45,891 --> 00:14:49,812
Talk about Beauty and the Beast.
She's both.

141
00:14:53,857 --> 00:14:57,778
The fourth skin-job is Pris,
a basic pleasure model.

142
00:14:57,945 --> 00:15:01,365
The standard item for military clubs
in the outer colonies.

143
00:15:02,991 --> 00:15:06,787
They were designed to copy human beings
in every way except their emotions.

144
00:15:06,954 --> 00:15:09,039
The designers reckoned
after a few years...

145
00:15:09,206 --> 00:15:11,708
...they might develop
their own emotional responses.

146
00:15:12,709 --> 00:15:15,295
Oh, hate, love, fear, anger, envy.

147
00:15:15,462 --> 00:15:17,714
So they built in a fail-safe device.

148
00:15:17,881 --> 00:15:21,218
- Which is what?
- Four-year lifespan.

149
00:15:27,266 --> 00:15:30,894
Now, there's a Nexus 6
over at the Tyrell Corporation.

150
00:15:31,061 --> 00:15:35,232
- I want you to go put the machine on it.
- And if the machine doesn't work?

151
00:17:06,865 --> 00:17:07,950
Do you like our owl?

152
00:17:11,453 --> 00:17:13,163
It's artificial?

153
00:17:14,164 --> 00:17:16,124
Of course it is.

154
00:17:17,459 --> 00:17:18,794
Must be expensive.

155
00:17:21,004 --> 00:17:23,507
Very. I'm Rachael.

156
00:17:24,466 --> 00:17:25,467
Deckard.

157
00:17:27,970 --> 00:17:32,015
It seems you feel our work
is not a benefit to the public.

158
00:17:32,975 --> 00:17:37,145
Replicants are like any other machine:
They're either a benefit or a hazard.

159
00:17:37,312 --> 00:17:40,065
If they're a benefit,
it's not my problem.

160
00:17:40,232 --> 00:17:43,110
May I ask you a personal question?

161
00:17:44,736 --> 00:17:46,488
Sure.

162
00:17:50,492 --> 00:17:52,828
Have you ever retired a human
by mistake?

163
00:17:55,163 --> 00:17:56,164
No.

164
00:17:56,331 --> 00:18:00,168
- But in your position, that is a risk.
- Is this to be an empathy test?

165
00:18:01,503 --> 00:18:05,048
Capillary dilation
of the so-called blush response?

166
00:18:05,674 --> 00:18:08,927
Fluctuation of the pupil.

167
00:18:09,845 --> 00:18:12,931
Involuntary dilation of the iris.

168
00:18:14,349 --> 00:18:16,310
We call it Voight-Kampff for short.

169
00:18:17,477 --> 00:18:19,521
Mr. Deckard, Dr. Eldon Tyrell.

170
00:18:20,522 --> 00:18:22,441
Demonstrate it.

171
00:18:23,275 --> 00:18:24,610
I wanna see it work.

172
00:18:24,776 --> 00:18:27,404
- Where's the subject?
- I wanna see it work on a person.

173
00:18:27,571 --> 00:18:30,532
I wanna see a negative
before I provide you with a positive.

174
00:18:30,699 --> 00:18:32,367
What's that gonna prove?

175
00:18:33,410 --> 00:18:35,370
Indulge me.

176
00:18:35,537 --> 00:18:37,039
On you?

177
00:18:37,205 --> 00:18:38,540
Try her.

178
00:18:43,920 --> 00:18:46,173
It's too bright in here.

179
00:19:23,502 --> 00:19:25,545
Do you mind if I smoke?

180
00:19:26,213 --> 00:19:27,589
It won't affect the test.

181
00:19:29,758 --> 00:19:32,928
All right, I'm gonna ask you
a series of questions.

182
00:19:33,095 --> 00:19:36,515
Just relax and answer them
as simply as you can.

183
00:19:44,022 --> 00:19:47,275
It's your birthday.
Someone gives you a calfskin wallet.

184
00:19:47,442 --> 00:19:49,236
I wouldn't accept it.

185
00:19:49,403 --> 00:19:53,824
Also, I'd report the person
who gave it to me to the police.

186
00:19:54,950 --> 00:19:58,286
You've got a little boy.
He shows you his butterfly collection...

187
00:19:58,453 --> 00:20:00,288
...plus the killing jar.

188
00:20:03,792 --> 00:20:05,293
I'd take him to the doctor.

189
00:20:11,299 --> 00:20:12,676
You're watching television.

190
00:20:12,843 --> 00:20:15,554
Suddenly you realize
there's a wasp crawling on your arm.

191
00:20:15,762 --> 00:20:17,973
I'd kill it.

192
00:20:20,809 --> 00:20:24,479
You're reading a magazine. You come
across a full-page nude photo of a girl.

193
00:20:24,646 --> 00:20:27,816
Is this testing whether I'm a Replicant
or a lesbian, Mr. Deckard?

194
00:20:27,983 --> 00:20:30,318
Just answer the questions, please.

195
00:20:34,781 --> 00:20:36,366
You show it to your husband.

196
00:20:36,533 --> 00:20:39,161
He likes it so much
he hangs it on your bedroom wall.

197
00:20:39,327 --> 00:20:40,662
...bush outside your window?

198
00:20:40,829 --> 00:20:42,038
I wouldn't let him.

199
00:20:42,205 --> 00:20:43,874
...orange body, green legs?

200
00:20:44,040 --> 00:20:45,333
Why not?

201
00:20:45,500 --> 00:20:47,669
I should be enough for him.

202
00:20:51,381 --> 00:20:53,383
One more question.

203
00:20:54,176 --> 00:20:57,220
You're watching a stage play,
a banquet is in progress.

204
00:20:57,387 --> 00:21:01,516
The guests are enjoying
an appetizer of raw oysters.

205
00:21:01,683 --> 00:21:04,811
The entrée consists of boiled dog.

206
00:21:21,369 --> 00:21:23,955
Would you step out
for a few moments, Rachael?

207
00:21:33,590 --> 00:21:35,258
Thank you.

208
00:21:36,510 --> 00:21:38,887
She's a Replicant, isn't she?

209
00:21:39,054 --> 00:21:40,806
I'm impressed.

210
00:21:40,972 --> 00:21:43,642
How many questions
does it usually take to spot one?

211
00:21:43,850 --> 00:21:46,561
- I don't get it, Tyrell.
- How many questions?

212
00:21:46,728 --> 00:21:49,397
Twenty, thirty, cross-referenced.

213
00:21:49,648 --> 00:21:52,108
It took more than a hundred for Rachael,
didn't it?

214
00:21:52,275 --> 00:21:54,110
She doesn't know.

215
00:21:54,277 --> 00:21:56,071
She's beginning to suspect, I think.

216
00:21:56,238 --> 00:21:58,323
Suspect?
How can it not know what it is?

217
00:21:58,865 --> 00:22:01,660
Commerce is our goal here at Tyrell.

218
00:22:01,868 --> 00:22:04,079
"More human than human"
is our motto.

219
00:22:04,246 --> 00:22:07,916
Rachael is an experiment,
nothing more.

220
00:22:08,083 --> 00:22:12,504
We began to recognize in them
a strange obsession.

221
00:22:12,671 --> 00:22:16,091
After all, they are emotionally
inexperienced, with only a few years...

222
00:22:16,258 --> 00:22:19,761
...in which to store up the experiences
which you and I take for granted.

223
00:22:19,928 --> 00:22:25,433
If we gift them with a past, we create
a cushion or a pillow for their emotions...

224
00:22:25,600 --> 00:22:28,436
...then consequently
we can control them better.

225
00:22:28,603 --> 00:22:30,438
Memories.

226
00:22:30,605 --> 00:22:32,524
You're talking about memories.

227
00:22:42,784 --> 00:22:45,328
<i>Reaction
time is a factor, so please pay attention.</i>

228
00:22:45,495 --> 00:22:47,455
<i>- Now, answer as quickly as you can.
- Sure.</i>

229
00:22:47,622 --> 00:22:50,292
<i>- 1187 at Hunterwasser.
- Yeah. That's the hotel.</i>

230
00:22:50,458 --> 00:22:51,626
<i>- What?
- Where I live.</i>

231
00:22:51,793 --> 00:22:53,461
<i>- Nice place?
- Yeah, sure, I guess.</i>

232
00:22:53,628 --> 00:22:55,463
<i>- Is that part of the test?
- No.</i>

233
00:23:25,994 --> 00:23:28,121
Kowalski.

234
00:25:15,270 --> 00:25:17,647
Time...

235
00:25:17,814 --> 00:25:19,065
...enough.

236
00:25:37,125 --> 00:25:40,086
Did you get your precious photos?

237
00:25:42,881 --> 00:25:45,091
Someone was there.

238
00:25:45,675 --> 00:25:47,469
Men?

239
00:25:51,139 --> 00:25:54,601
Policemen?

240
00:26:43,858 --> 00:26:45,360
Aha.

241
00:27:26,734 --> 00:27:29,946
<i>Fiery the angels fell</i>

242
00:27:30,113 --> 00:27:33,408
<i>Deep thunder rolled around their shores</i>

243
00:27:33,783 --> 00:27:37,120
<i>Burning with the fires of Orc</i>

244
00:27:39,914 --> 00:27:43,418
You not come here! Illegal!

245
00:27:46,296 --> 00:27:47,922
Hey! Hey!

246
00:27:49,257 --> 00:27:52,468
Cold! Those are my eyes! Freezing!

247
00:28:03,104 --> 00:28:04,772
Yes.

248
00:28:04,939 --> 00:28:06,441
Questions.

249
00:28:13,364 --> 00:28:15,617
Hey! Hey!

250
00:28:28,671 --> 00:28:33,218
Morphology. Longevity. Incept dates.

251
00:28:33,384 --> 00:28:34,886
Don't know.

252
00:28:35,053 --> 00:28:37,347
I don't know such stuff.

253
00:28:39,265 --> 00:28:41,184
I just do eyes.

254
00:28:41,351 --> 00:28:44,187
Just eyes, just genetic design.

255
00:28:44,354 --> 00:28:46,189
Just eyes.

256
00:28:48,066 --> 00:28:50,318
You Nexus, huh?

257
00:28:50,485 --> 00:28:53,071
I design your eyes.

258
00:28:53,279 --> 00:28:55,240
Chew...

259
00:28:55,406 --> 00:29:00,495
...if only you could see
what I've seen with your eyes.

260
00:29:00,662 --> 00:29:02,580
Now...

261
00:29:02,789 --> 00:29:04,582
...questions.

262
00:29:04,791 --> 00:29:06,834
I don't know answers.

263
00:29:07,001 --> 00:29:08,836
Who does?

264
00:29:12,340 --> 00:29:13,716
Tyrell.

265
00:29:13,883 --> 00:29:15,301
He knows everything.

266
00:29:17,178 --> 00:29:19,514
Tyrell Corporation?

267
00:29:19,681 --> 00:29:23,726
He big boss. Big genius.
He design your mind.

268
00:29:23,893 --> 00:29:25,812
Your brain, huh?

269
00:29:26,938 --> 00:29:28,690
Smart.

270
00:29:30,525 --> 00:29:32,610
Very cold.

271
00:29:34,737 --> 00:29:38,366
Not an easy man to see...

272
00:29:39,200 --> 00:29:41,619
Give me coat.

273
00:29:42,829 --> 00:29:44,122
...I guess?

274
00:29:49,460 --> 00:29:51,379
Sebastian.

275
00:29:52,046 --> 00:29:55,425
He take you there.
He take you there.

276
00:29:55,591 --> 00:29:58,136
Sebastian who?

277
00:30:00,555 --> 00:30:03,433
J.F...

278
00:30:03,599 --> 00:30:06,561
...Sebast....

279
00:30:08,146 --> 00:30:09,981
Now...

280
00:30:12,442 --> 00:30:14,485
...where...

281
00:30:14,652 --> 00:30:17,739
...would we find this...

282
00:30:17,905 --> 00:30:21,367
...J.F. Sebastian?

283
00:30:21,951 --> 00:30:24,162
<i>Let's continue, shall we?</i>

284
00:30:24,370 --> 00:30:26,414
<i>Describe in single words...</i>

285
00:30:26,581 --> 00:30:30,251
<i>...only the good things that come
into your mind about your mother.</i>

286
00:30:30,418 --> 00:30:32,587
<i>- My mother?
- Yeah.</i>

287
00:30:32,754 --> 00:30:34,339
<i>I'll tell you about my mother.</i>

288
00:30:56,402 --> 00:30:59,489
<i>Voice-print identification.
Your floor number, please.</i>

289
00:30:59,655 --> 00:31:03,701
- Deckard, 97.
<i>- Ninety-seven. Thank you.</i> Danke.

290
00:31:24,722 --> 00:31:26,974
I wanted to see you.

291
00:31:32,355 --> 00:31:34,065
So I waited.

292
00:31:34,232 --> 00:31:36,234
Let me help you.

293
00:31:39,320 --> 00:31:41,572
What do I need help for?

294
00:31:43,449 --> 00:31:45,743
I don't know why
he told you what he did.

295
00:31:45,952 --> 00:31:46,994
Talk to him.

296
00:31:47,578 --> 00:31:49,831
He wouldn't see me.

297
00:32:02,093 --> 00:32:04,011
You want a drink?

298
00:32:06,681 --> 00:32:08,099
No?

299
00:32:14,272 --> 00:32:16,858
You think I'm a Replicant, don't you?

300
00:32:22,572 --> 00:32:24,198
Look.

301
00:32:24,991 --> 00:32:27,702
It's me with my mother.

302
00:32:28,703 --> 00:32:30,371
Yeah?

303
00:32:36,502 --> 00:32:39,130
Remember when you were six?

304
00:32:39,297 --> 00:32:42,967
You and your brother snuck into an empty
building through a basement window.

305
00:32:43,134 --> 00:32:45,511
You were gonna play doctor?

306
00:32:46,387 --> 00:32:49,974
He showed you his, and when it got
to be your turn, you chickened and ran.

307
00:32:50,141 --> 00:32:51,809
Remember that?

308
00:32:54,061 --> 00:32:58,399
Did you ever tell anybody that?
Your mother, Tyrell, anybody, huh?

309
00:33:00,568 --> 00:33:03,821
You remember the spider that lived
in the bush outside your window?

310
00:33:04,572 --> 00:33:06,949
Orange body, green legs?

311
00:33:07,116 --> 00:33:09,911
Watched her build a web all summer?

312
00:33:10,077 --> 00:33:12,455
Then one day, there's a big egg in it.

313
00:33:12,622 --> 00:33:14,332
The egg hatched....

314
00:33:14,540 --> 00:33:17,084
- The egg hatched...
- And?

315
00:33:17,460 --> 00:33:20,421
...and a hundred baby spiders
came out.

316
00:33:21,631 --> 00:33:22,965
And they ate her.

317
00:33:27,762 --> 00:33:29,597
Implants.

318
00:33:29,764 --> 00:33:32,475
Those aren't your memories,
they're somebody else's.

319
00:33:32,642 --> 00:33:35,102
They're Tyrell's niece's.

320
00:33:43,110 --> 00:33:44,779
Okay.

321
00:33:44,946 --> 00:33:46,531
Bad joke.

322
00:33:46,697 --> 00:33:48,282
I made a bad joke.

323
00:33:48,449 --> 00:33:50,117
You're not a Replicant.

324
00:33:50,284 --> 00:33:51,327
Go home.

325
00:33:51,494 --> 00:33:53,162
Okay?

326
00:33:55,331 --> 00:33:58,459
No, really. I'm sorry.

327
00:33:58,960 --> 00:34:00,711
Go home.

328
00:34:13,724 --> 00:34:14,809
Want a drink?

329
00:34:16,727 --> 00:34:18,980
I'll get you a drink.

330
00:34:19,146 --> 00:34:21,482
I'll get a glass.

331
00:38:05,539 --> 00:38:07,041
Hey!

332
00:38:12,213 --> 00:38:14,423
You forgot your bag.

333
00:38:24,934 --> 00:38:26,894
I'm lost.

334
00:38:28,270 --> 00:38:30,231
Don't worry, I won't hurt you.

335
00:38:34,401 --> 00:38:35,611
What's your name?

336
00:38:35,778 --> 00:38:39,114
- Pris.
- Mine's J.F. Sebastian.

337
00:38:39,281 --> 00:38:41,367
- Hi.
- Hi.

338
00:38:44,662 --> 00:38:46,747
Oh, where were you going?

339
00:38:48,791 --> 00:38:50,376
Home?

340
00:38:51,669 --> 00:38:53,587
I don't have one.

341
00:38:57,007 --> 00:38:59,260
We scared each other pretty good,
didn't we?

342
00:38:59,426 --> 00:39:01,178
We sure did.

343
00:39:06,684 --> 00:39:08,978
I'm hungry, J.F.

344
00:39:09,436 --> 00:39:11,814
I got stuff inside.

345
00:39:12,273 --> 00:39:13,691
You wanna come in?

346
00:39:13,899 --> 00:39:16,193
I was hoping you'd say that.

347
00:39:50,352 --> 00:39:52,521
Do you live in this building
all by yourself?

348
00:39:52,688 --> 00:39:55,858
Yeah, I live here
pretty much alone right now.

349
00:39:56,442 --> 00:39:59,028
No housing shortage around here.

350
00:40:01,071 --> 00:40:03,657
Plenty of room for everybody.

351
00:40:12,666 --> 00:40:15,002
Watch out for the water.

352
00:40:16,879 --> 00:40:19,840
Must get lonely here, J.F.

353
00:40:21,842 --> 00:40:24,553
Not really. I make friends.

354
00:40:24,720 --> 00:40:28,098
They're toys. My friends are toys.
I make them.

355
00:40:28,265 --> 00:40:30,601
It's a hobby. I'm a genetic designer.

356
00:40:30,768 --> 00:40:32,686
Do you know what that is?

357
00:40:32,853 --> 00:40:34,396
No.

358
00:40:36,357 --> 00:40:37,858
Now.

359
00:40:39,526 --> 00:40:41,195
Oh.

360
00:40:42,529 --> 00:40:45,366
Yoo-hoo, home again.

361
00:40:50,371 --> 00:40:53,123
Home again, home again, jiggidy-jig.

362
00:40:53,290 --> 00:40:56,377
- Good evening, J.F.
- Evening, fellas.

363
00:41:08,138 --> 00:41:10,557
They're my friends. I made them.

364
00:41:10,724 --> 00:41:12,559
Where are your folks?

365
00:41:12,726 --> 00:41:14,228
I'm sort of an orphan.

366
00:41:14,395 --> 00:41:16,605
Oh. What about your friends?

367
00:41:16,772 --> 00:41:19,233
I have some, but I have to find them.

368
00:41:19,400 --> 00:41:22,027
I'll let them know
where I am tomorrow.

369
00:41:22,820 --> 00:41:24,238
Oh?

370
00:41:25,906 --> 00:41:30,244
Can I take those things for you?
They're soaked, aren't they?

371
00:43:09,259 --> 00:43:11,929
Enhance 224 to 176.

372
